# Chapter 8 · The Women's Movement in Pakistan, Phase by Phase

> This is the heart of the topic. It answers "Write a note on the women's movement in Pakistan" (2016), "Discuss in detail feminist movements in Pakistan" (2022), "APWA and WAF" (short note, 2018) and the first half of 2024 and 2026. Pakistani scholars **Khawar Mumtaz and Farida Shaheed** wrote the standard history, *Women of Pakistan: Two Steps Forward, One Step Back?* (==1987==).

## 8.1  The phases at a glance

::: fig timeline | The women's movement in Pakistan: six phases.
@layout vertical
== Phase 1 · Welfare and reform (1947–1970)
1949: APWA founded by Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan
1955: Protests over PM Bogra's second marriage; Rashid Commission
1961: Muslim Family Laws Ordinance
1965: Fatima Jinnah contests the presidential election
== Phase 2 · Constitutional equality (1971–1977)
1973: Constitution — Articles 25, 27, 34; reserved seats
1975: International Women's Year; Pakistan Women's Rights Committee
== Phase 3 · Resistance under Zia (1977–1988)
1979: Hudood Ordinances
1981: WAF founded after the Fehmida–Allah Bux case
1983: 12 February protest in Lahore; Safia Bibi case
1984: Qanun-e-Shahadat (Law of Evidence)
== Phase 4 · Democracy, Beijing and NGOs (1988–1999)
1988: Benazir Bhutto — first woman prime minister of a Muslim country
1995: Beijing conference; 1996: CEDAW accession
== Phase 5 · Reforms and machinery (2000–2017)
2000: NCSW; 2002: 60 reserved seats in the NA
2006: Protection of Women Act
2010–2016: laws on harassment, acid, anti-women practices, honour killing
== Phase 6 · New activism (2018–)
2018: First Aurat March; digital campaigns
2021: Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act
2025: Islamabad Child Marriage Restraint Act
:::

## 8.2  Phase 1 · Welfare and reform (1947–1970)

::: define APWA | the All Pakistan Women's Association
Founded in ==1949== by **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan** (its first president), wife of the first prime minister. It grew from Partition relief work into a national, non-political **welfare** organisation: schools, colleges, vocational centres, health clinics and handicraft industries for women.
-> MCQs 2016, 2018 and 2019 asked its founder, first president and year.
:::

- **Family law.** In ==1955== Prime Minister **Muhammad Ali Bogra** took a second wife. APWA and other women protested; the government set up the **Rashid Commission on Marriage and Family Laws** (report ==1956==). Its recommendations led to the **Muslim Family Laws Ordinance 1961** under Ayub Khan: registration of marriages, the arbitration council's permission for a second marriage, notice procedure for divorce, and the right of orphaned grandchildren to inherit.
- **Politics.** The **1956 Constitution** reserved seats for women. In ==1965== **Fatima Jinnah** challenged Ayub Khan for the presidency; some religious scholars supported a woman candidate in the circumstances. She lost amid claims of rigging.
- **Character of the phase:** elite-led, welfare-oriented, cooperative with the state — the pattern Mumtaz and Shaheed call working "within" the system.

## 8.3  Phase 2 · Constitutional equality (1971–1977)

- The **1973 Constitution** promised equality: **Article 25** (no discrimination on the basis of sex), **Article 27** (public service), **Article 32** (women in local government), **Article 34** (full participation of women), **Article 35** (protection of the family).
- Women were appointed to high offices: **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan** became Governor of Sindh (==1973==); women entered the foreign service and other cadres.
- **International Women's Year (1975)**: Pakistan set up a Women's Rights Committee.

## 8.4  Phase 3 · Resistance under Zia-ul-Haq (1977–1988)

General **Zia-ul-Haq**'s "Islamisation" brought laws that many women saw as discriminatory.

::: law Hudood Ordinances | February 1979
Five ordinances on **zina** (adultery and rape), theft, drinking, and false accusation. Under the **Zina Ordinance**, a woman who alleged rape but could not prove it with the strict evidence required could herself be charged with **zina** — having confessed to intercourse. Thousands of women were imprisoned.
-> MCQs 2016 and 2019: enacted in **1979** under **Zia-ul-Haq**. MCQ 2025: the Hudood Ordinances were a major focus of the 1980s women's movement.
:::

::: define WAF | the Women's Action Forum
Founded in **Karachi in September 1981**, soon with chapters in Lahore, Islamabad and Peshawar, after a court sentenced **Fehmida and Allah Bux** to death by stoning for zina (they had married against her family's wishes). A **non-hierarchical, non-party, secular** platform of women's organisations and individuals, WAF challenged the Hudood laws, the proposed **Law of Evidence**, separate women's universities and restrictions on women in sport and media.
:::

::: case 12 February 1983, Lahore | the defining protest
Members of **WAF** and the **Punjab Women Lawyers' Association** marched to the Lahore High Court against the proposed **Law of Evidence**, under which two women's testimony would equal one man's in some cases. Police **baton-charged and tear-gassed** them; many were arrested. The poet **Habib Jalib** joined them. Since ==2012== Pakistan has marked **12 February as National Women's Day**.
:::

- The **Safia Bibi** case (==1983==): a young, visually impaired woman who became pregnant after rape was convicted of zina, while the accused were acquitted. Public outcry led the Federal Shariat Court to overturn the conviction.
- The **Qanun-e-Shahadat Order 1984** (Law of Evidence) was enacted, with Article 17 requiring two women witnesses in financial agreements.
- **Qisas and Diyat** laws (1990, made permanent in 1997) later allowed families to "forgive" killers — a loophole in honour killings.
- **Pakistan Commission on the Status of Women** (==1983==–==85==) under **Zari Sarfaraz** recommended reforms; its report was shelved.
- Rural women joined the **Sindhiani Tehreek** (from ==1982==) against military rule.

::: fig compare | APWA and WAF — the short note of 2018.
## APWA (1949)
- Founder: Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan
- Aim: welfare, education, skills, health
- Method: service, cooperation with the state
- Politics: non-political, reformist
- Base: elite women; charity model
- Gains: schools, colleges, MFLO 1961 campaign
## WAF (1981)
- Founded in Karachi after the Fehmida–Allah Bux case
- Aim: resist discriminatory laws; women's rights
- Method: protest, lobbying, legal analysis, media
- Politics: secular, non-party, confrontational with Zia
- Base: professional urban women; activists, lawyers
- Gains: resisted Hudood laws; built a rights movement
:::

## 8.5  Phase 4 · Democracy, Beijing and NGOs (1988–1999)

- **Benazir Bhutto** became the first woman prime minister of a Muslim-majority country (==1988==). Her governments created the **First Women Bank** (==1989==) and **women's police stations** (==1994==), and launched the **Lady Health Worker Programme** (==1994==); but the Hudood laws were not repealed.
- Pakistan took part in **Beijing** (==1995==) and **acceded to CEDAW** (==1996==).
- **NGOs multiplied** — Shirkat Gah (==1975==), Aurat Foundation (==1986==), Simorgh, and many donor-funded groups — doing research, legal aid and advocacy (Chapter 10).
- The **HRCP** (==1987==) documented violence against women; the killing of **Samia Sarwar** in her lawyer's office (Lahore, ==1999==) brought honour killing to national attention.

## 8.6  Phase 5 · Reforms and machinery (2000–2017)

::: table | Laws and institutions of Phase 5.
@widths 1.4,12.6
Year | Law or institution
==2000== | **National Commission on the Status of Women** (NCSW)
==2000–01== | Devolution plan: **33%** seats for women in local government
==2002== | **60** reserved women's seats in the National Assembly and 17 in the Senate; National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women
==2004== | Criminal Law (Amendment) Act — first law naming "honour" crimes
==2006== | **Protection of Women (Criminal Laws Amendment) Act** — moved rape from the Zina Ordinance to the Penal Code
==2008== | Women's Parliamentary Caucus; **Fahmida Mirza** first woman Speaker; Benazir Income Support Programme
==2010== | Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act
==2011== | Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act; Prevention of Anti-Women Practices Act
==2016== | Anti-honour-killing amendment (October) and anti-rape amendment; Punjab Protection of Women against Violence Act
==2017== | Elections Act — 5% of general-seat tickets for women; election void if women's turnout below 10% in a constituency
:::

This phase shows **state feminism**: women's organisations, women legislators and commissions working through Parliament. The killing of social-media star **Qandeel Baloch** by her brother (July ==2016==) spurred Parliament to close the "forgiveness" loophole in honour killings.

## 8.7  Phase 6 · New activism (2018 onwards)

- The **Aurat March** (from ==8 March 2018==, first in Karachi, then Lahore, Islamabad and other cities) — young, urban, organised online; demands on violence, harassment, workers' rights, *khawaja sira* rights and **bodily autonomy**.
- The **Women Democratic Front** (==2018==) and **Hum Auratein** link feminism with labour and left politics.
- **Digital activism** and #MeToo (Chapter 4).
- New laws: **Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act 2021**; **Enforcement of Women's Property Rights Act 2020**; **Islamabad Child Marriage Restraint Act 2025** (age ==18==); **Islamabad Domestic Violence Act 2025**.
- **Backlash**: legal petitions to ban the march, counter-marches ("Haya March"), online abuse.

## 8.8  The other actors: beyond APWA, WAF and the Aurat March

The movement was never only a few urban organisations. Five other groups of actors shaped it.

### (1) Rural and working women

- **Sindhiani Tehreek** (from ==1982==), linked to the Awami Tehreek, organised thousands of rural Sindhi women against military rule, feudal power and for education — one of the few mass rural women's movements in South Asia.
- In the **Okara** military farms dispute (from ==2000==), women tenants defended their land rights, forming groups that confronted police and paramilitary forces.
- The **Lady Health Workers**, organised as a union, protested in Islamabad for regular jobs and pay; the Supreme Court ordered their regularisation in ==2012==.
- **Home-based workers' unions** won the **Sindh Home-Based Workers Act 2018**; women of fishing communities organised through the Pakistan Fisherfolk Forum.

### (2) Religious women's organisations

- The women's wing of the **Jamaat-e-Islami** has long organised women for education, welfare and politics within an Islamist framework, and has opposed parts of the secular feminist agenda.
- **Al-Huda** (founded ==1994== by **Farhat Hashmi**) runs Qur'an courses that reached large numbers of urban middle-class women. Scholars debate whether such piety movements empower women (by giving them religious knowledge and public roles) or reinforce conservative norms.

### (3) Artists, writers and performers

- Poets **Kishwar Naheed** ("We Sinful Women") and **Fahmida Riaz** challenged the 1980s restrictions in verse.
- The theatre group **Tehrik-e-Niswan** (founded ==1979== by **Sheema Kermani**) and **Ajoka** (founded ==1984== by **Madeeha Gauhar**) staged plays on women's rights.
- Filmmaker **Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy** won Oscars for *Saving Face* (==2012==, acid attacks) and *A Girl in the River* (==2016==, honour killing); the second is credited with adding pressure for the honour-killing law of ==2016==.

### (4) Lawyers and human-rights defenders

**Asma Jahangir** and **Hina Jilani**, with colleagues, founded the first all-women law firm (==1980==) and **AGHS Legal Aid**; Asma Jahangir co-founded the **Human Rights Commission of Pakistan** (==1987==) and defended women in Hudood cases. Women lawyers turned protest into litigation and legislation.

### (5) Women in Parliament and the state

Women legislators — especially after the ==2002== reserved seats — formed the cross-party **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** (==2008==) and moved many of the laws of 2010–2016. Commissions (NCSW, provincial commissions) and ombudspersons became allies inside the state.

::: simple
Pakistan's women's movement is a **network**: elite welfare workers, secular activists, rural peasants, health workers, religious women, poets, lawyers and legislators. They often disagree — but most major gains came when several of them pushed at once.
:::

::: fig pyramid | How the focus of Pakistan's movement has shifted over time.
Welfare and education (1947–1970): APWA
Constitutional equality (1970s): 1973 Constitution
Legal resistance (1980s): WAF against the Hudood laws
Policy and machinery (1990s–2010s): NGOs, NCSW, new laws
Bodily autonomy and voice (2018–): Aurat March, digital activism
:::

::: eye | Answering "Discuss in detail feminist movements in Pakistan" (2022)
Give a definition and the Mumtaz–Shaheed history in the introduction; then one heading per **phase** (six), each with dates, organisations, leaders, laws and a line of evaluation; then a heading on **actors** (APWA, WAF, NGOs, rural movements, the state, the Aurat March); then **strengths, weaknesses and the way forward** (Chapter 11). Draw the timeline or the pyramid.
:::
